import{_ as t,o,c as r,a3 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"2.6 Empty Storage? Full Storage? We Need More Oversupply!","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"2.6 空仓？满仓？我们需要更多的加压！","archivedAt":"2026-10-29","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=2kENbD6F4B8qcMtdPdudqt","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-2kENbD6F4B8qcMtdPdudqt.md"}'),s={name:"2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply.md"};function n(a,e,l,d,c,p){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="_2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply" tabindex="-1">2.6 Empty Storage? Full Storage? We Need More Oversupply! <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-6-empty-storage-full-storage-we-need-more-oversupply" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2\\.6 Empty Storage? Full Storage? We Need More Oversupply!&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>2kENbD6F4B8qcMtdPdudqt</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=2kENbD6F4B8qcMtdPdudqt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>Original section title: “Transferring Blocking Signals through the Depot: Oversupply, Full-Storage and Empty-Storage Balancing.”</p><blockquote><p><em>“Full-storage balancing is 区.”</em></p><p>— 2026/8: while building maximum gross production in 1.4, 言 discovered Xiranite production failed to reach 360 and said this.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p><em>“Empty-storage balancing is 区.”</em></p><p>— 2026/9: while building maximum gross production in 1.5, 言 discovered Xiranite unloaders were not outputting Xiranite and said this.</p><p><em>“Full-storage balancing is not 区!”</em></p><p>— 2026/10: while building maximum gross production in 1.6, 言 discovered that our techniques seemed to have completely overcome the biggest problem of full-storage balancing, and said this.</p></blockquote><p>This subsection discusses transferring blocking signals through the depot, unloaders, and loaders when lines are spatially separate without direct logistics connections, **even across different sub-bases.<br> **<span class="mark-grey">#Comment 言: In practice, constrained by liquid/gas resources, footprint, the limit on pipe components, the device cap, and the pipe cap, it is very hard to complete every processing step in a single base starting from primary raw materials, so for automatic control the need to transfer blocking across bases is very common.</span></p><p>First review Depot Unloader and Depot Loader behavior. <s>I planned to link the basic factory knowledge here, but it is still blank.</s></p><p>Never mind: their priorities are still not fully understood. There may be rules, but they may change offline. <a href="/endfield-skydoc/depot-related-priority-phenomena">Depot-Related Priority Phenomena</a> For now we assume unloaders service outputs round-robin. There is some evidence for ordered loader priorities, but with sufficient storage capacity they can also be treated as round-robin.<br><span class="mark-grey">#Proofreading 言: “sufficient storage capacity” → “the corresponding item is not at full storage”</span></p><p>We can therefore treat the depot and its unloaders as a <strong>giant splitter or splitting network</strong>, applying Section 2.5 directly. Suppose we want <u>120 Ferrium per minute entering the depot to supply 73.75 for Xircon and 47.25 for Yazhen Syringe [C]</u>. Give the Xircon module 3 Ferrium unloaders and the medicine module 2.</p><p>While <span class="mark-purple"><strong>Ferrium remains in storage</strong></span>, every unloader supplies full rate, an effective maximum total supply of 150. In the earlier notation, the Xircon module receives 90- and medicine 60+. <u>Once blocking forms, Xircon actually receives 73.75</u>. The excess 26.25 redirects to medicine, but that module is designed to consume only 60 Ferrium. That 26.25 must therefore remain in storage; medicine can also be treated as blocked. Total Ferrium consumption reaches 133.75, exceeding production and gradually reducing reserves.</p><p>Once <span class="mark-purple"><strong>Ferrium runs out</strong></span>, splitter behavior becomes apparent. No unloader reaches full rate, because <strong>only 120 Ferrium enters the depot per minute, shared equally between 5 unloaders</strong>. Xircon receives 72- and medicine 48+. <strong>Xircon&#39;s Ferrium supply is now insufficient</strong> to form blocking: R=3/5=0.6, while R*=73.75/120=0.614583.</p><p>What now? Add more unloaders for Xircon. With 4 for Xircon and still 2 for medicine, R becomes 4/6=0.6667; Xircon receives 80- and medicine 40+. This suffices to block Xircon production and redirect the excess to medicine.</p><blockquote><p>To calculate the actual medicine flow, first subtract the blocked controlled branch&#39;s 73.75 from total Ferrium production 120. The remaining 46.25 is shared equally between its two output ports.</p></blockquote><p>Providing more than production/30 unloaders overall, and more than the module&#39;s planned production rate/30 unloaders for that module, is called <strong>oversupply</strong>. It generally draws depot reserves down to 0, yet continues supplying enough inputs after unloader speeds fall below 30, preserving the <strong>blocked controlled branch</strong>.</p><blockquote><p>Since consumption exceeds production, reaching zero reserves forces consumption to equal production. The depot&#39;s accumulation rate may be written 0+. Until the connected modules block completely, this 0+ effectively remains 0. This is <strong>empty-storage balancing</strong>.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>I favor a broader definition: if a location&#39;s stable balanced rate is x, deliberately use priority or splitting to initially send more than x there, especially a larger proportion, making it block more quickly. That too is oversupply.</p></blockquote><p>Designs for the first blocking type often detect a full-storage signal. Traditionally, inputs first enter a stash—or a machine if processing is needed—whose priority output returns to the depot and secondary output feeds further production. <strong>The controlled branch is depot storage; the free branch is downstream production.</strong> Only full depot storage sends material toward the Moulding Unit for production.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-3641791cf99b67f2.q90-e6c5d6f8372b2916.webp" alt="26 Empty Storage? Full Storage? We Need More Oversupply!"></p><p>However, <span class="mark-red"><strong>this has a major problem: the detector&#39;s return-to-depot flow takes deposit capacity away from newly produced material</strong></span>. In the illustrated example, full Cuprium storage triggers Cuprium Bottle production. In 1.5, 17 Refining Units deposit Cuprium. The detector effectively adds another deposit route. The stash is disabled; the route is a <strong>Depot Loader</strong>. For simplicity, consider <u>a hypothetical model with equal-weight round-robin servicing of loaders</u>.</p><ol><li>Initially, <u>other lines produce 510 and consume 480 Cuprium/min</u>. Before storage fills, the detector withdraws 30 Cuprium/min and redeposits 30 Cuprium/min.</li><li>Once storage fills, Cuprium must be withdrawn and consumed to admit more. Since <u>production exceeds consumption while the Moulding Unit is inactive, every loader blocks</u>.</li><li>Let x of the detector&#39;s flow divert to the Moulding Unit, leaving 30-x returning to the depot. Total consumption is now 480+x.</li><li>Ideally x=30. <strong>But the external Refining Units and detector all deposit together</strong>, and all are blocked. In the equal-sharing model, the space freed by consumption 480+x is shared among 18 inlets, each receiving (480+x)/18 deposit flow.</li><li>Therefore 30-x=(480+x)/18, giving x=3.16. The detector barely serves its intended purpose; instead it blocks Refining Units, which can process only 17*(30-3.16)=456 Cuprium Ore.</li></ol><blockquote><p>Another blocking-graph calculation.</p></blockquote><p>Actual loader priorities change the numbers somewhat, but the problem remains awkward.</p><p>Suppose we somehow give the detector&#39;s return loader <strong>lowest priority</strong>:</p><ul><li>If other lines uniformly withdraw 16 Cuprium every 2 seconds, and this device uniformly withdraws 1 every 2 seconds, the 17 Refining Units can always deposit 17 every 2 seconds. The detector&#39;s loader deposits nothing; all excess goes to the Moulding Unit.</li><li>But if other lines fluctuate, withdrawing 28 in the first 2 seconds and 4 in the next 2 of every 4-second cycle, the long-term average remains 480/min. After withdrawing 28 in the first 2 seconds, even the lowest-priority loader can deposit, still displacing normal production.</li></ul><blockquote><p>Why would withdrawal fluctuate? Is someone secretly oversupplying again?</p></blockquote><ul><li>If all production deposits through stashes, their buffers fill vacancies quickly. Nevertheless, while stashes await transfer back to storage, the detector&#39;s loader still deposits and displaces normal production.</li><li>Using a stash for the full-storage detector and loaders on the production side greatly alleviates this, leaving some competition only at the instant the stash transfers back.</li></ul><p>To eliminate or avoid these problems, consider:</p><ol><li>Detect an item with no other producing line depositing it. For example, divert one Cuprium line to Cuprium Part and detect that; full-storage blocking then sends Cuprium elsewhere. If no other line deposits Cuprium Part, there is no competition.</li><li>Fully understand stash/loader priorities, with offline stability too.</li><li>Use more complex signal-control structures, still under development; see other pages.</li><li>(Not yet checked) <a href="/endfield-skydoc/conventional-priority-architectures">Conventional Priority Architectures (Not Structures)</a></li></ol><p>In any case, <strong>unloader behavior is not yet fully understood</strong>. Be cautious with full- and empty-storage designs and check the arithmetic carefully. Even 言&#39;s automatic lines encounter mysterious unloaders that output nothing at empty storage. I recommend practicing with simple lines before large-scale use.</p><p><s>Personally, I mostly favor full-rate unloaders: storage neither empty nor full.</s></p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/2-5-is-a-stash-not-just-a-special-splitter">2.5 Is a Stash Not Just a Special Splitter? (Core Content ⭐)</a></p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/production-control-and-frontier-research">Production Control and Frontier Research</a></p><p>Translation note: The quoted slang 区 is retained as ambiguous in the source. Ferrium here is the author&#39;s abbreviated material name. The split figure and the later flow figure in the source differ, and both are kept as written; the mismatch is not silently corrected.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
